//? Title: ID3: Categorical Questions and Information Gain
//? Subtitle: Exact entropy accounting, greedy stopping and frozen category dictionaries
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A classifier must turn a row of categorical attributes into a class while choosing
which questions to ask and when to stop. Many different trees fit the same rows,
and a perfect fit can come from memorising identifiers. ID3 grows a multiway tree
by maximising the immediate reduction in empirical class entropy. A four-row
calculation and the fourteen-row Play Tennis fixture expose every split. XOR and
unique identifiers show two different failures of this implementation's positive-gain
rule. A fixed Titanic holdout illustrates training-only category dictionaries,
local-majority fallback and the difference between training fit and prediction.
)
\toc

> The method

>> From class frequencies to a categorical question

Let \(S\) contain \(n\) labelled rows and let \(p_c=n_c/n\). A question about
attribute \(A\) partitions rows into \(S_v=\{i:x_{iA}=v\}\). Integer codes stand
for categories: their numerical distances and order have no meaning. ID3 creates
one branch per observed value, rather than a binary threshold. Define

$$
 H(S)=-\sum_{c:n_c>0}p_c\log_2p_c,\qquad
 H(S\mid A)=\sum_v\frac{n_v}{n}H(S_v),\qquad
 G(S,A)=H(S)-H(S\mid A).
$$
\alttext(Class entropy is minus the sum of class probabilities times their base-two logarithms. Conditional entropy weights each child entropy by its fraction of rows. Information gain is parent entropy minus conditional entropy, in bits.)

Pure nodes have zero entropy. Concavity of entropy gives
\(H(\sum_v w_vp_{c\mid v})\ge\sum_vw_vH(p_{c\mid v})\), hence
\(0\le G\le H(S)\) in exact arithmetic. Equivalently the gain is the empirical
mutual information between the class and that attribute. A useful question reduces
expected uncertainty; the algorithm compares this immediate reduction at the current
node, without searching all future trees.

The retained TikZ source illustrates a four-row question. Its build needs LaTeX with
TikZ, amsmath and lmodern, plus dvisvgm. Normal documentation exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, file="figures/id3_information_gain.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/id3_information_gain.tikz > build/id3_information_gain.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/id3_information_gain.svg build/id3_information_gain.dvi > build/id3_information_gain.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the four-row information-gain diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the four-row information-gain diagram.
// \image(figures/id3_information_gain.svg)
// result_end
\caption(A question partitions the rows; its score depends on the weighted uncertainty left in the children.)
\alttext(A parent with three no and one yes splits into a pure two-no child and a one-no one-yes child. Each has weight one half. Conditional entropy is half a bit and information gain is 0.311278 bits.)

>> State the actual stopping and prediction rules

At a mixed node, `id3_train` tests each unused attribute and chooses the first
strict maximum in feature-index order. It stores the node's majority label, then
recurses over observed values only if the best computed gain is positive and at
least two branches exist. It stops at purity, exhausted attributes, the depth cap,
or a nonpositive best gain. This positive-gain restriction is an implementation
choice; it is stronger than permitting arbitrary zero-gain ID3 splits. Computed
gain ties choose the lowest feature index. Majority ties choose the smallest integer
label. There is no floating-point gain tolerance.

Root depth is zero. `max_depth=1` permits one question, `2` permits two, and the
special value `0` means unlimited depth. A path uses each attribute at most once,
so its split depth is at most the number of attributes. Contradictory labels on
identical feature rows remain unresolved. No pruning or missing-value inference is
performed. Aligned empty training inputs yield an empty tree; prediction on it is
rejected. Zero-column nonempty inputs yield a majority leaf. Ragged or misaligned
training arrays are rejected.

Prediction follows category edges until a leaf. For a value unseen *at the current
node*, it immediately returns that node's stored majority; it does not search a
similar branch. A level can exist elsewhere in the training data and still be absent
at that particular node. `id3_trace` shows exactly where traversal stops.

>> What a split can and cannot guarantee

For a tree whose internal nodes are \(\mathcal I\), empirical gains telescope:

$$
 H(S)-\sum_{\ell\in\mathcal L}\frac{n_\ell}{n}H(S_\ell)
 =\sum_{t\in\mathcal I}\frac{n_t}{n}G(S_t,A_t).
$$
\alttext(The root entropy minus weighted leaf entropy equals the sum of internal-node information gains weighted by their fractions of all training rows.)

Every positive-gain split lowers weighted leaf entropy. Replacing a majority leaf
by majority-labelled children also cannot increase training mistakes: each child
could keep the parent's label, and its own majority does at least as well. Thus
allowing deeper prefixes of the same deterministic tree cannot reduce training
accuracy. Neither fact guarantees smaller test error, a globally smallest tree,
or even the possibility of reaching a useful split after a zero-gain first question.

At a node, this ordered-map implementation partitions each candidate attribute and
counts classes. A conservative upper bound is \(O(ndh\log n)\) work over a tree of
split depth \(h\le d\), rather than a threshold-search CART complexity. Every leaf
contains at least one fitting row, giving at most \(n\) leaves and \(2n-1\) nodes
when all internal nodes have at least two children. Stored recursive row partitions
can occupy \(O(nh)\) space, besides the output tree and attribute bookkeeping.
Prediction costs \(O(h\log b)\) for at most \(b\) category branches per visited node.

> Small examples

>> Four rows: calculate both gains and finish the tree

Take rows \((A,B)=(0,0),(0,1),(1,0),(1,1)\) with labels
\((0,0,1,0)\), where zero means no and one means yes. Then
\(H(S)=h_2(1/4)=0.8112781245\) bits. Either first question leaves one pure pair
and one balanced pair, so both gains equal \(0.8112781245-1/2\).
The lower-index attribute \(A\) wins. Its \(A=1\) child needs \(B\) to separate
its two labels, while its \(A=0\) child is already pure. The resulting five-node
tree fits all four rows.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/id3_four_rows.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace a = dm::algorithms;
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> x{{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}};
  const std::vector<int>              y{0, 0, 1, 0};
  const auto                          splits  = a::id3_split_table(x, y, {"A", "B"});
  const auto                          tree    = a::id3_train(x, y);
  auto                                entropy = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(
                     {splits.double_at("entropy", 0),
                                                     splits.double_at("conditional_entropy", 0),
                                                     splits.double_at("gain", 0)},
                     {"parent", "after A", "gain"},
                     "bits",
                     {50, 100, 190})
                     .title("One question's entropy accounting")
                     .x_label("entropy quantity")
                     .y_label("bits")
                     .y_limits(0, 1);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(entropy)
      .add(a::plot_id3_tree(tree, {"A", "B"}))
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/id3_four_rows.svg");
  std::print(
      "Candidate questions\n\n{}\n\nBranches of A\n\n{}\n\nTraining evaluation\n\n{}\n",
      splits.to_md(),
      a::id3_branch_table(x, y, 0).to_md(),
      a::id3_evaluate(tree, x, y).to_md());
}
```
