> Checking the theory

>> XOR: uncertainty can require a zero-gain first question

For all four binary pairs with labels \(Y=A\mathbin{\mathrm{xor}}B\), the parent
entropy is one bit. Conditioning on either attribute leaves a balanced pair on each
branch, so both gains are zero. This implementation stops at a majority leaf,
chooses class zero by its tie rule, and fits only two rows. Yet the two-question
rule “ask A, then B” fits every row. This is a limitation of immediate positive-gain
stopping, not an impossibility of representing XOR with a decision tree.

>> Identifiers: maximal gain can mean memorisation

For six rows let \(Y=(0,0,0,1,1,1)\), an imperfect signal
\(A=(0,0,0,0,1,1)\), and unique row identifiers \(I=0,1,\ldots,5\).
The signal has gain \(1-(4/6)h_2(1/4)=0.459148\) bits. Each identifier branch
is pure, giving gain one bit. ID3 chooses the identifier and memorises all six rows.
On new identifiers it returns the tied root majority, class zero, even when the
signal suggests class one. No statistic here estimates a population error rate.
The two new rows are a deliberately constructed probe.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/id3_greedy_limits.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace a = dm::algorithms;
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> xor_x{{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 0}, {1, 1}};
  const std::vector<int>              xor_y{0, 1, 1, 0};
  const auto                          xor_tree = a::id3_train(xor_x, xor_y);
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> id_x{{0, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, 2}, {0, 3}, {1, 4}, {1, 5}};
  const std::vector<int>              id_y{0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1};
  const auto                          id_tree = a::id3_train(id_x, id_y);
  const auto gains    = a::id3_split_table(id_x, id_y, {"signal", "identifier"});
  auto       xor_plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                      .points({0, 1}, {0, 1}, "class 0", {40, 100, 190}, 10)
                      .points({0, 1}, {1, 0}, "class 1", {200, 80, 40}, 10)
                      .title("XOR: both first gains are zero")
                      .x_label("A")
                      .y_label("B")
                      .x_limits(-.25, 1.25)
                      .y_limits(-.25, 1.65)
                      .legend({"class 0", "class 1"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {200, 80, 40}});
  auto id_plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(
                     gains.numeric_column("gain").values(),
                     {"signal", "identifier"},
                     "gain (bits)",
                     {50, 100, 190})
                     .title("Unique identifiers win immediate gain")
                     .x_label("candidate attribute")
                     .y_label("gain (bits)")
                     .y_limits(0, 1.15);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2)
      .add(xor_plot)
      .add(id_plot)
      .size(1100, 490)
      .fit_panels()
      .save_svg("build/id3_greedy_limits.svg");
  std::print(
      "XOR root gains\n\n{}\n\nXOR training fit\n\n{}\n\nIdentifier root gains\n\n{}\n\nIdentifier "
      "training fit\n\n{}\n\nNew identifier probe\n\n{}\n",
      a::id3_split_table(xor_x, xor_y, {"A", "B"}).to_md(),
      a::id3_evaluate(xor_tree, xor_x, xor_y).to_md(),
      gains.to_md(),
      a::id3_evaluate(id_tree, id_x, id_y).to_md(),
      a::id3_evaluate(id_tree, {{0, 6}, {1, 7}}, {0, 1}).to_md());
}
