\caption(Zero immediate gain blocks the XOR tree, whereas a unique identifier creates pure training leaves and fails to generalise to new identifiers.)
\alttext(Left: four XOR corners with diagonally equal classes. Right: gain is 0.459148 for the imperfect signal and one bit for the identifier. Tables show XOR accuracy one half, memorised training accuracy one, and new-identifier probe accuracy one half.)

>> Depth caps preserve prefixes; unseen values use local majorities

The tennis root alone fits 10/14 rows; allowing a second question fits all fourteen.
Further depth caps leave the same eight nodes. This checks the nondecreasing training
accuracy argument on a finite fixture. It does not say that deeper trees improve
prediction. The flat root-majority baseline would fit 9/14.

Now query sunny weather with humidity code 99. The root recognises sunny, then the
humidity test has no matching edge and returns the sunny subset's majority, no.
For outlook code 99 the root itself falls back to yes. Code 99 is an explicit novel
category, not a missing-value model. The trace table includes the final unseen stop
and each visited node's fallback label.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/id3_depth_and_fallback.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  namespace a           = dm::algorithms;
  const auto          d = a::id3_tennis();
  std::vector<double> depth, accuracy, nodes;
  for (std::size_t cap = 1; cap <= 4; ++cap) {
    const auto t = a::id3_train(d.features, d.labels, cap);
    depth.push_back(cap);
    accuracy.push_back(a::id3_evaluate(t, d.features, d.labels).double_at("accuracy", 0));
    nodes.push_back(t.nodes.size());
  }
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame table;
  table.add_column("max_depth", depth);
  table.add_column("training_accuracy", accuracy);
  table.add_column("nodes", nodes);
  auto fit = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                 .line(depth, accuracy, "tree", {40, 100, 190})
                 .points(depth, accuracy, "", {40, 100, 190}, 5)
                 .line({1, 4}, {9. / 14, 9. / 14}, "majority", {180, 100, 40})
                 .title("Depth and training accuracy")
                 .x_label("maximum split depth")
                 .y_label("accuracy")
                 .x_limits(.8, 4.2)
                 .y_limits(.55, 1.2)
                 .legend({"tree", "majority"}, {{40, 100, 190}, {180, 100, 40}});
  auto size = dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
                  .line(depth, nodes, "", {40, 100, 190})
                  .points(depth, nodes, "", {40, 100, 190}, 5)
                  .title("The tree stops growing at depth two")
                  .x_label("maximum split depth")
                  .y_label("nodes")
                  .x_limits(.8, 4.2)
                  .y_limits(0, 9);
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(fit).add(size).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/id3_depth_and_fallback.svg");
  const auto tree = a::id3_train(d.features, d.labels);
  std::print(
      "Depth profile\n\n{}\n\nSunny, unseen humidity\n\n{}\n\nUnseen outlook\n\n{}\n",
      table.to_md(),
      a::id3_trace(tree, {0, 0, 99, 0}).to_md(),
      a::id3_trace(tree, {99, 0, 0, 0}).to_md());
}
```
