\image(figures/id3_titanic.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The Titanic tree uses only fitting-row dictionaries and gains. Both accuracy baselines predict the fitted root majority; the holdout never selects a depth or predictor.)
\alttext(Left: fitting-row information gains for Sex, Pclass and Embarked. Right: tree and fitted-majority accuracy for 711 fitting rows and 178 holdout rows, with a fixed maximum depth of two.)

The fitted tree classifies 550/711 fitting passengers and 149/178 holdout passengers
correctly; the fitted-majority baseline classifies 108/178 holdout passengers correctly.
The root asks Sex and both children next ask Pclass, but every female leaf still
predicts survival and every male leaf predicts non-survival. Consequently the extra
question reduces class entropy without changing any hard prediction relative to the
depth-one tree; the program checks equality on the holdout. The holdout accuracy is
higher than the fitting accuracy in this particular split, illustrating sample
variation rather than a guarantee about new passengers.

The root gain table and fitted node table expose what was learned. The reported
fallback counts refer to missing *branches*, which need not be globally novel levels.
Accuracy is unweighted by passenger, and the baseline class comes from the fitting
root even when evaluating the holdout. Training and holdout performance quantify
this one declared experiment; they are not causal effects of sex, class or embarkation
and do not establish class-probability calibration. The model returns hard labels,
not estimated survival probabilities.

> Conclusion

The four-row calculation connects class counts, weighted child entropy and a complete
tree. Play Tennis shows that a multiway root can ask different questions on different
branches, while the depth experiment checks its deterministic prefixes. XOR exposes
this variant's refusal to take a zero-gain first step; unique identifiers expose
information gain's preference for training purity with many categories. An unseen
branch has a precise, inspectable local-majority fallback.

The Titanic example applies those same rules through frozen training dictionaries,
with an explicitly fixed depth and holdout. ID3 remains a greedy categorical tree
without pruning, smoothing, missing-value inference or a guarantee of generalisation.
The neighbouring `c45_train` routine implements continuous binary gain-ratio tests;
it is a restricted C4.5-inspired variant, not the full C4.5 system or a drop-in
categorical ID3 alternative.

> References

1. J. R. Quinlan (1986), “Induction of decision trees”, *Machine Learning* 1, 81–106.
   [Publisher record](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00116251),
   [original paper](https://storm.cis.fordham.edu/~gweiss/selected-papers/quinlan86.pdf).
   The report distinguishes the local implementation's positive-gain stopping rule.
2. [Kaggle, Titanic — Machine Learning from Disaster](https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/titanic/data).
   Bundled dataset provenance and missingness are documented by `dm::datasets::titanic()`;
   the Play Tennis data are a separate constructed teaching fixture.

